# Line endings are load-bearing here, not cosmetic.
#
# This tree is built and audited on WSL, but it is also checked out on Windows,
# and the two disagree. A CRLF inside a shell script makes bash reject the very
# first line it cares about:
#
#     scripts/wsl-build.sh: line 8: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# The build then dies before doing any work, and nothing in the code says why.
# That is not hypothetical: re-attributing the commit history on WSL rewrote
# the scripts with CRLF and took the build down, because core.autocrlf had been
# set to true to stop WSL git reporting every text file as modified.
#
# core.autocrlf cannot express what this repository needs - LF for the scripts
# that run under bash, CRLF only for the Windows launchers - so it is stated
# here, where it travels with the repository and applies on every platform
# regardless of each machine's own settings.

# Scripts interpreted by a POSIX shell: must be LF on every platform.
*.sh     text eol=lf
gradlew  text eol=lf

# Windows launchers: CRLF is what cmd.exe expects.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# Build outputs and vendored binaries are never touched.
*.apk    binary
*.aar    binary
*.jar    binary
*.jks    binary
*.keystore binary
